2. Normative Commitments and Empirical Claims

2.1 Normative Axiom

The framework adopts one value commitment:

\boxed{\text{Technological acceleration must not require human disempowerment.}}

Operationally, this requires:

F_t \ge F_{\min}

where F_t is a freedom index and F_{\min}=0.70 on the V-Dem Liberal Democracy Index. Trajectories that fall below F_{\min} are classified as non-viable, even if they produce high technological capability.

This commitment is not testable. It is a boundary condition imposed by the researcher.

2.2 Empirical Claims

The following claims are empirical and can be false:

· AI capability can increase aggregate human capability under appropriate institutions.
· Democratic systems recover from technological and macroeconomic shocks at least as quickly as comparable non-democratic systems.
· Space-directed R&D generates measurable total factor productivity spillovers to terrestrial production.
· Increasing extraterrestrial autonomy reduces modeled civilizational extinction risk.

None of these claims is assumed in advance. In particular, the space-spillover claim is now stated cautiously in light of Kantor and Whalley (2025), which finds localized sectoral gains but limited broad TFP spillovers from space-race federal R&D.

