4. The Stochastic Dynamical System

All equations include stochastic disturbances:

\varepsilon_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\Sigma)

where \Sigma may include nonzero off-diagonal elements. For viability analysis, disturbances are truncated to a bounded support to preserve state bounds.

Normalization convention: All index variables are dimensionless and bounded in [0,1]. Physical variables—energy, compute, labor, capital—are normalized by their base-year reference values when entering the production function or the extinction-risk function. Coefficient units are understood under this normalization.

4.1 Energy Capacity

E_{t+1} = E_t + \eta_E u_{E,t} - \delta_E E_t + \varepsilon_{E,t}

where

· \eta_E is energy capacity added per unit of investment,
· \delta_E is depreciation and retirement.

AI electricity demand is:

E_{AI,t} = \xi_{\text{train}} K_{c,t} + \xi_{\text{infer}} A_t^{\omega} Y_t

where

· \xi_{\text{train}} is electricity demand per unit of AI compute capacity,
· \xi_{\text{infer}} is electricity demand per unit of AI-serviced output,
· \omega > 0.

Calibration note: E_t is dispatchable capacity in TWh/year. E_{AI,t} is consumption in TWh/year. The coefficients \xi_{\text{train}} and \xi_{\text{infer}} absorb the capacity-to-consumption conversion. Based on IEA (2025) and EPOCH data, \xi_{\text{train}} is calibrated to reproduce current training electricity demand, while \xi_{\text{infer}} is calibrated so that inference demand grows at the IEA-projected 30% annual rate.

Total electricity constraint:

g_1(x_t) = E_t - E_{AI,t} - E_{base,t} \ge 0

4.2 AI Compute Capacity

K_{c,t+1} = K_{c,t} + \eta_{Kc} u_{Kc,t} - \delta_{Kc} K_{c,t} + \varepsilon_{Kc,t}

4.3 Usable Data Stock

D_{t+1} = D_t + \left[\alpha_D S_t Y_t + \eta_{\text{syn}} A_t\right](1-D_t)
- \lambda_D R_t D_t
- \delta_D D_t
+ \varepsilon_{D,t}

where

· \alpha_D captures new usable data generated by scientific and economic activity,
· \eta_{\text{syn}} captures synthetic data generation,
· \lambda_D captures diminishing value from repeated training,
· \delta_D is data depreciation or obsolescence.

Correction from v0.3: The term -\lambda_D K_{c,t}^{\rho}D_t has been replaced by -\lambda_D R_t D_t, where

R_t = \max\left(0, \frac{K_{c,t}^{\beta}/D_t}{\rho_{\text{opt}}} - 1\right)

is the overtraining ratio relative to the Chinchilla-optimal compute-to-data ratio \rho_{\text{opt}}. This matches the findings of Muennighoff et al. (2023) and Villalobos et al. (2022): repeated use degrades data value, but the constraint is not that compute destroys data; it is that excess compute relative to available unique data causes repetition decay.

This makes data endogenous and potentially scarce.

4.4 AI Capability

A_{t+1} = A_t + \left[\alpha_A K_{c,t}^{\beta} D_t^{\gamma} S_t^{\eta}\right](1-A_t)
- \delta_A A_t
+ \varepsilon_{A,t}

where

\beta, \gamma, \eta \in (0,1)

This form is bounded in [0,1] in the deterministic skeleton. It captures diminishing returns to compute, data, and scientific knowledge. It does not assume scaling continues indefinitely.

4.5 Human Capability

H_{t+1} = H_t
+ \left[\alpha_H u_{H,t-\ell_H} + \theta_1 \left(\frac{A_t}{A_0}\right)^{\rho} H_t\right](1-H_t)
- \theta_2 \left(\frac{A_t}{A_0}\right)^{\rho} H_t
- \delta_H H_t
+ \varepsilon_{H,t}

where

· \ell_H is the education and retraining lag, typically 12–16 years,
· \theta_1 captures AI augmentation of human capability,
· \theta_2 captures AI displacement or deskilling.

The net effect \theta_1 - \theta_2 determines whether AI raises or lowers effective human capability.

4.6 Labor Force

\ln L_{t+1} = \ln L_t + n_t
- \chi_L (A_t - A_0)_+
- \chi_H (H_{\min} - H_t)_+
+ \varepsilon_{L,t}

where (x)_+ = \max(x,0). This keeps L_t > 0 and allows AI-driven labor force exit.

4.7 Scientific Capability

S_{t+1} = S_t + \left[\alpha_S u_{S,t} + \gamma_S A_t H_t L_t\right](1-S_t)
- \delta_S S_t
+ \varepsilon_{S,t}

4.8 Infrastructure

I_{t+1} = I_t + \left[\alpha_I u_{I,t} + \gamma_I E_t S_t\right](1-I_t)
- \delta_I I_t
+ \varepsilon_{I,t}

4.9 Resilience

R_{t+1} = R_t + \left[\alpha_R u_{R,t} + \gamma_R I_t F_t\right](1-R_t)
- \delta_R R_t
- \lambda_R \frac{E_{AI,t}}{E_{AI,t} + E_{base,t}} R_t
+ \varepsilon_{R,t}

The term \lambda_R \frac{E_{AI,t}}{E_{AI,t} + E_{base,t}} R_t captures stress on grid and societal resilience from AI electricity demand. The denominator is bounded away from zero by construction, avoiding division-by-zero issues.

4.10 Freedom

F_{t+1} = F_t
+ \left[\alpha_F u_{F,t} + \gamma_F (F_0 - F_t)\right](1-F_t)
- \left[\beta_F H_{AI,t} (A_t - A_0)_+ + \delta_F\right] F_t
+ \varepsilon_{F,t}

where

· \beta_F captures centralizing pressure from AI capability growth,
· H_{AI,t} is the Herfindahl index of frontier AI compute ownership.

This is the critical correction: disempowerment pressure is driven by concentration of compute, not merely by AI capability. A widely distributed AI capability with low H_{AI,t} may not produce disempowerment pressure, while a concentrated AI capability with high H_{AI,t} does.

4.11 Extraterrestrial Autonomy

X_{t+1} = X_t + \left[\alpha_X u_{X,t} + \gamma_X S_t I_t\right](1-X_t)
- \delta_X X_t
+ \varepsilon_{X,t}

4.12 Non-AI Physical Capital

K_{t+1} = K_t + s Y_t - \delta_K K_t + \varepsilon_{K,t}

where s is the saving and investment rate. K_t is dynamic, not constant.

4.13 Total Factor Productivity

\ln Z_{t+1} = \ln Z_t + \gamma_Z S_t + \gamma_{\text{space}} u_{X,t-\ell_X} - \delta_Z + \varepsilon_{Z,t}

where

· \gamma_{\text{space}} is the lagged TFP spillover from space investment,
· \ell_X is the lag, typically 7–10 years.

Refinement from v0.3: \gamma_{\text{space}} is no longer a single parameter. It is split into two channels in Section 7:

\gamma_{\text{space}} = \gamma_{\text{launch}} + \gamma_{\text{science}}

where \gamma_{\text{launch}} captures TFP spillover from launch-cost reduction (e.g., Starlink-class services), and \gamma_{\text{science}} captures TFP spillover from NASA science and technology transfer. The science channel is expected to be small relative to terrestrial frontier R&D, based on Kantor and Whalley (2025).

