5. Task-Based Production with Time-Varying Elasticity of Substitution

The production function is:

Y_t = Z_t K_t^{\alpha}
\left[
\omega_{H,t} (L_t H_t)^{\frac{\sigma_t - 1}{\sigma_t}}
+
\omega_{A,t} (A_t K_{c,t}^{\beta})^{\frac{\sigma_t - 1}{\sigma_t}}
\right]^{\frac{\sigma_t (1-\alpha)}{\sigma_t - 1}}

where

\sigma_t = \sigma_0 + \sigma_1 A_t

and the task weights are dynamic:

\omega_{A,t} = \bar{\omega}_A \Gamma(A_t)

\omega_{H,t} = 1 - \omega_{A,t}

where \Gamma(A_t) is the share of tasks that are automatable or AI-exposed at capability level A_t. \Gamma(\cdot) is estimated from Eloundou et al. (2024) exposure scores and Gmyrek et al. (2025) automation/augmentation scores.

At \sigma_t=1, the CES function reduces to the Cobb-Douglas limit:

Y_t = Z_t K_t^{\alpha}
(L_t H_t)^{\omega_{H,t}(1-\alpha)}
(A_t K_{c,t}^{\beta})^{\omega_{A,t}(1-\alpha)}

This is required because the exponent \frac{\sigma_t-1}{\sigma_t} becomes zero.

This specification treats AI as a task input T_t = A_t K_{c,t}^{\beta}. The elasticity of substitution between human effective labor and AI task capacity is \sigma_t.

· If \sigma_t < 1, human labor and AI are complements.
· If \sigma_t > 1, they are substitutes.

The time-varying form allows the model to capture the transition from complementarity to substitution as AI capability improves.

The human labor income share is:

s_{H,t} =
\frac{
\omega_{H,t} (L_t H_t)^{\frac{\sigma_t - 1}{\sigma_t}}
}{
\omega_{H,t} (L_t H_t)^{\frac{\sigma_t - 1}{\sigma_t}}
+
\omega_{A,t} (A_t K_{c,t}^{\beta})^{\frac{\sigma_t - 1}{\sigma_t}}
}

This is the correct basis for testing whether AI enrichment comes at the expense of labor income. Because \omega_{A,t} is dynamic, the labor share does not collapse mechanically as K_{c,t} rises; it only collapses if the automatable task share \Gamma(A_t) rises faster than the augmentation effect on H_t.

