6. Viability Constraints and Extinction Risk

The constraint set is:

\mathcal{V} = \left\{ x \in \mathbb{R}^{n} \mid g_i(x) \ge 0, \ i = 1,\dots,m \right\}

with explicit constraints:

g_1 = E_t - E_{AI,t} - E_{base,t} \ge 0

g_2 = F_t - F_{\min} \ge 0

g_3 = H_t - H_{\min} \ge 0

g_4 = R_t - R_{\min} \ge 0

g_5 = P_{\max} - P_{ext,t} \ge 0

g_6 = E_t - (1+\mu) E_{AI,t} \ge 0

Clarification on g6: g6 is a reserve margin specifically scoped to AI load. It enforces that dispatchable capacity exceeds AI demand by a margin \mu=0.15, independent of non-AI base load. g1 is the total electricity balance. Both are required. g6 does not replace g1; it adds a stricter AI-specific reliability constraint.

Numerical thresholds (provisional):

· F_{\min} = 0.70 on the V-Dem Liberal Democracy Index.
· H_{\min} = 0.50 on the human capability index.
· R_{\min} = 0.50 on the resilience index.
· P_{\max} = 0.05 per decade.
· \mu = 0.15.

The extinction-risk function is:

P_{ext,t} =
\frac{1}{1 + \exp\left(\kappa_0 - \kappa_E \tilde{E}_t - \kappa_F F_t - \kappa_R R_t - \kappa_X X_t\right)}

where \tilde{E}_t = E_t / \bar{E} is normalized energy capacity, and the other inputs are already in [0,1]. The \kappa coefficients are calibrated so that baseline current conditions imply P_{ext,t} \approx 0.01, and a severe collapse in F_t, R_t, and E_t implies P_{ext,t} \to P_{\max}.

The stochastic viability kernel is defined as:

Viab_{\mathcal{F}}(\mathcal{V})
=
\left\{
x_0 \in \mathcal{V}
\mid
\exists u(\cdot) \in \mathcal{U}_{\text{ad}}(x)
\text{ such that }
x(t; x_0, u(\cdot), \varepsilon) \in \mathcal{V}
\text{ almost surely } \forall t \ge 0
\right\}

A state is viable if and only if at least one future policy path keeps the system inside the constraint set for all time, under the budget constraint and stochastic disturbances.

Numerical computation: The Saint-Pierre algorithm is infeasible in thirteen dimensions. The pilot computation therefore fixes H,S,I,R,X at steady-state values and computes the viability kernel on the (K_c,F,E) subspace only, with stochastic disturbances truncated to bounded support. This is a standard approach in high-dimensional viability analysis.

If the projected kernel is empty, that is a theorem-level result: there is no feasible investment path that maintains F_t \ge 0.70, E_t \ge (1+\mu)E_{AI,t}, and the remaining constraints under current energy and concentration trends.

