Appendix B

Boundedness of Index State Dynamics

The index states are intended to remain in [0,1]. This appendix states the conditions under which the deterministic skeleton preserves that property, and explains the numerical treatment when those conditions fail.

---

B.1 General Logistic Update

Consider an index state X_t \in [0,1] with dynamics

X_{t+1}
=
X_t
+
F_t(1-X_t)
-
G_t X_t,

where F_t \ge 0 and G_t \ge 0 are time-varying drift terms.

Proposition B.1.
If

F_t \in [0,1],
\qquad
G_t \in [0,1],
\qquad
F_t + G_t \le 1,

then the deterministic update maps [0,1] into [0,1].

Proof.
Rewrite the update as

X_{t+1}
=
F_t + X_t(1 - F_t - G_t).

Let X_t \in [0,1]. Since 1 - F_t - G_t \ge 0, the expression is nondecreasing in X_t. Therefore,

F_t
\le
X_{t+1}
\le
F_t + (1 - F_t - G_t)
=
1 - G_t.

Because F_t \ge 0 and G_t \ge 0, the lower bound is nonnegative and the upper bound is at most 1. Hence

X_{t+1} \in [0,1].

This completes the proof.

---

B.2 Application to the Model

For each index state, the drift terms are:

State F_t G_t
D_t \alpha_D S_t Y_t + \eta_{\text{syn}} A_t \lambda_D R_t + \delta_D
A_t \alpha_A K_{c,t}^{\beta} D_t^{\gamma} S_t^{\eta} \delta_A
H_t \alpha_H u_{H,t-\ell_H} + \theta_1 (A_t/A_0)^{\rho} H_t \theta_2 (A_t/A_0)^{\rho} + \delta_H
S_t \alpha_S u_{S,t} + \gamma_S A_t H_t L_t \delta_S
I_t \alpha_I u_{I,t} + \gamma_I E_t S_t \delta_I
R_t \alpha_R u_{R,t} + \gamma_R I_t F_t \delta_R + \lambda_R \frac{E_{AI,t}}{E_{AI,t}+E_{base,t}}
F_t \alpha_F u_{F,t} + \gamma_F(F_0 - F_t) \beta_F H_{AI,t}(A_t-A_0)_+ + \delta_F
X_t \alpha_X u_{X,t} + \gamma_X S_t I_t \delta_X

The sufficient condition F_t + G_t \le 1 is not guaranteed for all states because some drift terms depend on unbounded physical quantities such as Y_t, K_{c,t}, or L_t.

---

B.3 Numerical Treatment

For numerical implementation, two approaches are used:

1. Projection. After every stochastic update, states are projected onto [0,1] via

\Pi_{[0,1]}(x) = \min\{1, \max\{0, x\}\}.

2. Truncated stochastic disturbances. The Gaussian disturbances are truncated to a bounded support so that the projection is applied only to the deterministic drift, not to the noise.

The projection operator \Pi_{[0,1]} is monotone and nonexpansive, and therefore does not distort the qualitative dynamics in the interior of the state space.

Proposition B.2.
Let X_t \in [0,1]. Let \varepsilon_t be bounded with support [-\bar\varepsilon, \bar\varepsilon]. Then the projected update

X_{t+1}
=
\Pi_{[0,1]}
\left(
X_t + F_t(1-X_t) - G_t X_t + \varepsilon_t
\right)

maps into [0,1] almost surely.

Proof.
Immediate from the definition of \Pi_{[0,1]}. The projection guarantees the bound by construction.

