Appendix C

Task-Based CES Production Function: Details and Proofs

---

C.1 CES Aggregator and Outer Cobb-Douglas

Define the task aggregator:

Q_t =
\left[
\omega_{H,t} X_{H,t}^{\rho_t}
+
\omega_{A,t} X_{A,t}^{\rho_t}
\right]^{1/\rho_t},

where

X_{H,t} = L_t H_t,
\qquad
X_{A,t} = A_t K_{c,t}^{\beta},
\qquad
\rho_t = \frac{\sigma_t - 1}{\sigma_t}.

Production is

Y_t = Z_t K_t^{\alpha} Q_t^{1-\alpha}.

The inner aggregator has constant returns to scale. The outer capital exponent \alpha gives the capital share of total income.

---

C.2 Cobb-Douglas Limit as \sigma_t \to 1

Proposition C.1.
As \sigma_t \to 1, the CES aggregator converges to

Q_t
=
X_{H,t}^{\omega_{H,t}}
X_{A,t}^{\omega_{A,t}},

provided \omega_{H,t} + \omega_{A,t} = 1.

Proof.
When \sigma_t \to 1, \rho_t \to 0. Write

\ln Q_t
=
\frac{1}{\rho_t}
\ln
\left(
\omega_{H,t} X_{H,t}^{\rho_t}
+
\omega_{A,t} X_{A,t}^{\rho_t}
\right).

Because \omega_{H,t} + \omega_{A,t} = 1, the term inside the logarithm converges to 1 as \rho_t \to 0. Apply L'Hôpital's rule:

\lim_{\rho_t \to 0}
\frac{
\ln
\left(
\omega_H X_H^{\rho}
+
\omega_A X_A^{\rho}
\right)
}{\rho}
=
\frac{
\omega_H \ln X_H
+
\omega_A \ln X_A
}{
\omega_H + \omega_A
}
=
\omega_H \ln X_H + \omega_A \ln X_A.

Therefore,

\ln Q_t
\to
\omega_{H,t} \ln X_{H,t}
+
\omega_{A,t} \ln X_{A,t}.

Exponentiating gives

Q_t
=
X_{H,t}^{\omega_{H,t}}
X_{A,t}^{\omega_{A,t}}.

Substituting into Y_t = Z_t K_t^{\alpha} Q_t^{1-\alpha} yields the Cobb-Douglas limit:

Y_t
=
Z_t K_t^{\alpha}
(L_t H_t)^{\omega_{H,t}(1-\alpha)}
(A_t K_{c,t}^{\beta})^{\omega_{A,t}(1-\alpha)}.

---

C.3 Income Shares

Let the task-level labor share be

s_{H,t}^{\text{task}}
=
\frac{
\omega_{H,t} X_{H,t}^{\rho_t}
}{
\omega_{H,t} X_{H,t}^{\rho_t}
+
\omega_{A,t} X_{A,t}^{\rho_t}
}.

Then the labor share of total income is

s_{H,t}^{\text{total}}
=
(1-\alpha)
s_{H,t}^{\text{task}}.

The main text writes s_{H,t} without the (1-\alpha) factor because it is often used as the share of non-capital income. In all empirical work, the total labor share must include (1-\alpha).

Derivation.
Let Q_t be the inner CES aggregator. The marginal product of human effective labor is

\frac{\partial Y_t}{\partial X_{H,t}}
=
Z_t K_t^{\alpha}
(1-\alpha)
Q_t^{-\alpha}
\cdot
\frac{\partial Q_t}{\partial X_{H,t}}.

Since

\frac{\partial Q_t}{\partial X_{H,t}}
=
Q_t^{1-\rho_t}
\omega_{H,t}
X_{H,t}^{\rho_t - 1},

we have

\frac{\partial Y_t}{\partial X_{H,t}}
=
(1-\alpha)
Y_t
\cdot
\frac{
\omega_{H,t} X_{H,t}^{\rho_t}
}{
\omega_{H,t} X_{H,t}^{\rho_t}
+
\omega_{A,t} X_{A,t}^{\rho_t}
}
\cdot
\frac{1}{X_{H,t}}.

Multiplying by X_{H,t} and dividing by Y_t gives

s_{H,t}^{\text{total}}
=
(1-\alpha)
s_{H,t}^{\text{task}}.

---

C.4 Dynamic Task Weights

The task weights are

\omega_{A,t}
=
\bar{\omega}_A \Gamma(A_t),
\qquad
\omega_{H,t}
=
1 - \omega_{A,t},

where \Gamma(A_t) is the share of tasks that are AI-exposed or automatable at capability level A_t.

This specification ensures that as AI capability rises and more tasks become exposed, the effective weight on AI task input increases endogenously. This prevents the human labor share from collapsing mechanically merely because K_{c,t} rises; it collapses only if the automatable task share \Gamma(A_t) grows faster than the augmentation effect on H_t.

