Appendix D

Data Stock Dynamics and Repetition Decay

---

D.1 The Overtraining Ratio

The data stock equation is

D_{t+1}
=
D_t
+
\left[\alpha_D S_t Y_t + \eta_{\text{syn}} A_t\right](1-D_t)
-
\lambda_D R_t D_t
-
\delta_D D_t
+
\varepsilon_{D,t},

where R_t is the overtraining ratio:

R_t
=
\max
\left(
0,
\frac{K_{c,t}^{\beta}/D_t}{\rho_{\text{opt}}}
-
1
\right).

Here \rho_{\text{opt}} is the Chinchilla-optimal compute-to-data ratio from Hoffmann et al. (2022).

---

D.2 Motivation from Repeated Epoch Decay

Muennighoff et al. (2023) and Villalobos et al. (2022) find that repeated use of the same data degrades its marginal value. The effective data stock under repetition is approximately

D^{\text{eff}}_t
=
D_t
\exp\left(-\lambda_D R_t\right).

For small \lambda_D R_t, this is approximated by

D^{\text{eff}}_t
\approx
D_t (1 - \lambda_D R_t).

The state equation therefore includes the loss term -\lambda_D R_t D_t, which captures diminishing value from repeated training rather than the physically meaningless claim that compute destroys data.

---

D.3 Boundedness and Scarcity

The (1-D_t) factor in the data-creation term ensures that data stock approaches saturation as D_t \to 1. The repetition decay term ensures that data stock falls when the compute-to-data ratio exceeds the Chinchilla optimum.

In the limit where R_t = 0 and D_t \to 1,

D_{t+1}
\to
1 - \delta_D
+
\varepsilon_{D,t}.

Thus data cannot exceed 1 in the deterministic skeleton when the decay terms are included.

---

Appendix E

Energy Equations: Capacity, Consumption, and IEA Calibration

---

E.1 Capacity vs. Consumption

The state variable E_t is installed dispatchable electricity generation capacity, measured in TWh/year maximum output. Electricity consumption, including AI demand, is measured in TWh/year.

The key distinction is that capacity can exceed consumption, but consumption cannot exceed capacity in any period without reliability failure.

The AI electricity consumption is

E_{AI,t}
=
\xi_{\text{train}} K_{c,t}
+
\xi_{\text{infer}} A_t^{\omega} Y_t.

The first term captures training electricity demand, which scales with installed compute capacity. The second term captures inference electricity demand, which scales with AI-serviced output.

---

E.2 IEA 2025 Calibration

IEA (2025) reports:

· Data centers consumed approximately 415 TWh in 2024.
· Data centers are projected to consume approximately 945 TWh in 2030.
· Electricity consumption from servers used for AI workloads, predominantly inference, is projected to grow by approximately 30% annually.

This implies that inference demand is the fastest-growing AI electricity component. The split in the model is therefore not a stylistic choice; it is required to match IEA projections.

---

E.3 Calibration Targets

The coefficients \xi_{\text{train}} and \xi_{\text{infer}} are calibrated so that:

1. At current K_{c,t}, \xi_{\text{train}} K_{c,t} matches current training electricity consumption as reported by EPOCH.
2. At current A_t and Y_t, \xi_{\text{infer}} A_t^{\omega} Y_t matches current inference electricity consumption as reported by IEA.

A plausible inference coefficient derived from per-query energy estimates is

\xi_{\text{infer}}
\approx
0.24 \text{--} 0.30 \text{ watt-hours per query},

scaled by the number of queries per unit of normalized output.

---

E.4 Constraints g1 and g6

The total electricity balance is

g_1
=
E_t - E_{AI,t} - E_{base,t}
\ge
0.

The AI-specific reserve margin is

g_6
=
E_t - (1+\mu) E_{AI,t}
\ge
0,
\qquad
\mu = 0.15.

These are distinct constraints:

· g1 requires total capacity to exceed total demand.
· g6 requires capacity to exceed AI demand by a 15% reserve margin, independent of base load.

Neither implies the other. A system could satisfy g1 but violate g6 if AI demand is a large share of total demand and the reserve margin is not met for AI specifically.

