Appendix F

Viability Kernel: Definitions, Algorithms, and Projection

---

F.1 Deterministic Viability Kernel

For the deterministic skeleton with no stochastic disturbances, the viability kernel of the constraint set \mathcal{V} is

Viab_{\mathcal{V}}
=
\left\{
x_0 \in \mathcal{V}
\mid
\exists u(\cdot) \in \mathcal{U}_{\text{ad}}(x_0)
\text{ such that }
x(t; x_0, u(\cdot)) \in \mathcal{V}
\ \forall t \ge 0
\right\}.

A state is viable if there exists at least one policy path that keeps the system inside \mathcal{V} forever.

---

F.2 Stochastic Viability Kernel

With stochastic disturbances, the almost-sure viability kernel is

Viab_{\mathcal{F}}(\mathcal{V})
=
\left\{
x_0 \in \mathcal{V}
\mid
\exists u(\cdot) \in \mathcal{U}_{\text{ad}}(x_0)
\text{ such that }
x(t; x_0, u(\cdot), \varepsilon) \in \mathcal{V}
\text{ almost surely } \forall t \ge 0
\right\}.

For numerical work, the disturbances are truncated to a bounded support. The resulting kernel is a robust viability kernel under bounded noise.

---

F.3 Saint-Pierre Algorithm

The Saint-Pierre algorithm approximates the deterministic viability kernel by backward iteration:

1. Initialize K_0 = \mathcal{V}.
2. For n = 0, 1, 2, \dots, define

K_{n+1}
=
\left\{
x \in K_n
\mid
\exists u \in \mathcal{U}_{\text{ad}}(x)
\text{ such that }
x + \Delta t \cdot f(x,u) \in K_n
\right\}.

3. The limit K_{\infty} is the numerical viability kernel.

For the stochastic version, the condition is replaced by

x + \Delta t \cdot f(x,u) + \Delta t \cdot \varepsilon \in K_n

for all \varepsilon in the bounded support of the disturbance.

---

F.4 Curse of Dimensionality and Pilot Projection

The full state space is 13-dimensional. A grid with 10 points per dimension requires 10^{13} points, which is computationally infeasible.

Therefore, the pilot computation fixes

H_t, S_t, I_t, R_t, X_t

at their steady-state values and computes the viability kernel on the three-dimensional subspace

(K_c, F, E).

The projected kernel answers the question:

Does there exist an investment path in compute, energy, and institutional adaptation that keeps F_t \ge 0.70, E_t \ge (1+\mu)E_{AI,t}, and the fixed state constraints satisfied?

If the projected kernel is empty, then the full kernel is also empty, because the projection is a necessary condition for viability. Therefore, an empty projected kernel is a theorem-level negative result.

---

F.5 Pseudo-Code for the Pilot Projection

```
Input: parameter vector theta, initial grid G on (K_c, F, E)
Initialize K0 = points in G satisfying all constraints
for n = 0 to N:
    K_{n+1} = empty
    for each x in K_n:
        for each admissible control u on a finite control grid:
            for each disturbance eps in bounded support:
                x_next = x + dt * f(x,u) + dt * eps
                if x_next in K_n:
                    add x to K_{n+1}
                    break
return K_N
```

