Appendix G

Extinction Risk Calibration and Normalization

---

G.1 Normalized Logistic Function

The extinction-risk function is

P_{ext,t}
=
\frac{1}{1 + \exp\left(
\kappa_0
-
\kappa_E \tilde{E}_t
-
\kappa_F F_t
-
\kappa_R R_t
-
\kappa_X X_t
\right)}.

All inputs are normalized:

· \tilde{E}_t = E_t / \bar{E}
· F_t, R_t, X_t \in [0,1]

This prevents the logistic function from being dominated by the variable with the largest numerical scale.

---

G.2 Monotonicity

Proposition G.1.
If \kappa_E, \kappa_F, \kappa_R, \kappa_X > 0, then P_{ext,t} is strictly decreasing in each of \tilde{E}_t, F_t, R_t, X_t.

Proof.
Let

z_t
=
\kappa_0
-
\kappa_E \tilde{E}_t
-
\kappa_F F_t
-
\kappa_R R_t
-
\kappa_X X_t.

Then

P_{ext,t}
=
\frac{1}{1 + e^{z_t}}.

The derivative with respect to any input V \in \{\tilde{E}, F, R, X\} is

\frac{\partial P_{ext,t}}{\partial V}
=
-
\kappa_V
\frac{e^{z_t}}{(1+e^{z_t})^2}
<
0,

because \kappa_V > 0 and the logistic density is positive. Therefore, P_{ext,t} is strictly decreasing in each input.

---

G.3 Baseline Calibration

The constant \kappa_0 is chosen so that current baseline conditions imply

P_{ext,t} \approx 0.01.

The slope coefficients \kappa_E, \kappa_F, \kappa_R, \kappa_X are chosen so that a severe collapse in energy capacity, freedom, and resilience implies

P_{ext,t} \to P_{\max} = 0.05.

This is a provisional calibration. It does not require direct observation of extinction events; it requires expert elicitation and sensitivity analysis, as is standard in catastrophic-risk modeling.

