H.2 Available Data

U.S. annual data on frontier AI compute begins in approximately 2012–2015. Even if all state variables are observed, the usable time series has at most 10–15 annual observations.

---

H.3 Identifiability Theorem

Proposition H.1.
The full structural system in Section 4 is not jointly identifiable from available U.S. annual data.

Proof sketch.
A necessary condition for identification is that the number of independent moment conditions exceeds the number of free parameters. With at most 15 time-series observations and more than 40 free parameters, the parameter-to-observation ratio is greater than 8:3. Moreover, many parameters enter nonlinearly and interact multiplicatively. Standard rank and order conditions for nonlinear dynamic systems are therefore violated. No consistent joint estimator exists.

---

H.4 Resolution

The structural system is calibrated from literature-sourced elasticities, not jointly estimated. The four hypotheses H1–H4 are tested through reduced-form designs:

· H1: industry-level difference-in-differences
· H2: matched cross-country recovery comparisons
· H3: patent/citation-based spillover comparison
· H4: model-internal simulation ensembles

This distinction is fundamental. The structural model is a coherence device and scenario engine; it is not an estimation equation. The reduced-form tests carry the empirical burden.

